[32m+[m[32m## 3. DPDP Act 2023 Compliance Checklist (India)[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Why is this required?[m
[32m+[m[32mThis compliance checklist is an **explicit deliverable required for Issue #23 (`S0-DIM-1`)** in `docs/SPRINT_PLAN.md`. Under Indian law, the **Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 (DPDP Act 2023)** is the primary legal framework protecting individuals from the misuse of personal and health data.[m[41m [m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Protection Against Data Misuse[m
[32m+[m[32mThis checklist guarantees that user data cannot be exploited:[m
[32m+[m[32m1. **No Unauthorized Commercialization or Misuse:** The platform is legally and architecturally prohibited from selling health data, sharing it with advertisers, or using personal conversations to train public AI models.[m
[32m+[m[32m2. **Strict Accountability & Penalties:** Under Section 33 of the DPDP Act 2023, failure to implement reasonable security safeguards or misusing personal data carries statutory penalties of up to ₹250 crore imposed by the Data Protection Board of India.[m
[32m+[m[32m3. **Data Principal Empowerment:** Users are legally recognized as Data Principals with absolute rights to access, withdraw consent, and demand total erasure of their health data.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mAs a Data Fiduciary operating in India handling sensitive health data, our platform implements the following statutory obligations:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m| Section / Principle | Requirement | Implementation in System | Verification & Enforcement | Status |[m
[32m+[m[32m|---|---|---|---|---|[m
[32m+[m[32m| **Section 5 (Notice)** | Clear, plain-language itemized notice before collecting personal data. | Landing page and Consent Step present the exact purpose, data categories, retention terms, and rights before the user enters symptoms. | `ConsentStep.tsx` displays notice before `POST /consents`. | Compliant |[m
[32m+[m[32m| **Section 6 (Consent)** | Consent must be free, specific, informed, unconditional, and unambiguous with clear affirmative action. | Affirmative checkbox and explicit acceptance button. No pre-ticked checkboxes; no dark patterns; no bundled clauses. | `POST /consents` records `policy_version` and timestamp. | Compliant |[m
[32m+[m[32m| **Section 6(4) (Withdrawal)** | Right to withdraw consent easily with comparable ease. | "Delete My Data" action in user settings / footer purges the assessment and revokes consent. | `DELETE /api/v1/privacy/data` cascades removal. | Compliant |[m
[32m+[m[32m| **Section 7 (Purpose Limitation)** | Data used solely for specified triage purposes. | Symptom data is processed strictly to determine triage urgency and condition relevance. Never used for marketing, ad profiling, or model training. | Architectural boundary: LLM API calls are stateless and opt out of training retention. | Compliant |[m
[32m+[m[32m| **Section 8(1) (Data Minimization)** | Collect only what is strictly necessary. | Guest mode requires zero identity data (no phone, no email). Only symptom text and duration are collected. | Guest flow functions completely without user registration. | Compliant |[m
[32m+[m[32m| **Section 8(5) (Storage Limitation)** | Data must be erased once the purpose is served. | Unregistered guest assessments are automatically purged after 24 hours via a scheduled TTL cleanup job (`S2-DIM-4`). | Database TTL purge query deletes orphaned records `created_at < NOW() - INTERVAL '24 hours'`. | Compliant |[m
